This page has no mathematics. It is a small torn slip of green paper with an archivist's or editor's note, not in Ramanujan's hand. It reads: "with Add Ms a 94^18 (56)" in the top right, then "This is 10. p50 but from section 5 onwards is not published".

What the note refers to: the previous folio (Add. MS a94/18, f.56) is Ramanujan's manuscript on the infinite product phi(alpha,beta) = prod_{n>=1} {1+((alpha+beta)/(n+alpha))^3}. Its numbered parts are:
(1) a factorisation of the n-th factor of phi(alpha,beta)phi(beta,alpha) into linear and quadratic pieces;
(2) a Gamma-function evaluation of the linear part;
(3) a sine/cosh product evaluation of the quadratic part;
(4) the resulting closed form for phi(alpha,beta)phi(beta,alpha);
(5) the consequence that prod_{k>=1} {1+(x/(n+k))^3} is expressible in finite terms when x-2n is an integer.

This matches Ramanujan's paper "On the product prod_{n=0}^infty [1+(x/(a+nd))^3]" (J. Indian Math. Soc. 7 (1915), 209-211). That is paper no. 10 in the Collected Papers of Srinivasa Ramanujan (Hardy, Seshu Aiyar, Wilson, eds., 1927), beginning on p. 50. The cataloguer, plausibly G. N. Watson or a later Trinity librarian, records that the manuscript corresponds to Collected Papers no. 10, p. 50. The material from section 5 onwards (the finite-terms consequence and whatever follows) did not appear in the published version.

The next folio (on the series E(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2! + ... and dE/dx = E) appears to be an unrelated sheet in the same bundle. There is no derivation on this page to reconstruct.
